494:**Memory in a designed workflow.** Agents do not use memory well on their own: their tool loads a fixed set of
504:| **Context** — memory in | The entries the node needs, named by id or by the paths they cover, and how they reach it: in the brief, by an import, or by a start hook, never as a sentence asking it to read them (SoT Rd2.1). What it must not receive: a verifier gets no worker's reasoning, and no entry or journal that carries it. |
505:| **Contract** — before acting | A node that acts on a decision or fact checks it against the code first; a mismatch is reported in the state file and to EJ, not settled (SoT Rd5). |
506:| **State** — memory out | What the node writes, and when: a decision or fact before the code it governs (W5.1); `done:` on a decision when its work is complete (W5.3); raw notes to the task's `journal/`, turned into lessons, observations and hypotheses at the end of the task (K5.2); `INDEX.md` regenerated in the same commit as any entry (K6.2). The node's return lists the entries it wrote, and its check fails if a required one is missing. |
507:| **Tools** — the caches | A cache is never loaded at start (CACHES Rd1). A node uses full-text search or the vector index only for a free-text "what do we know about X" question, and only when that cache exists (its trigger fired, B1); otherwise `INDEX.md`, grep, then read the entry. Exact strings, "every entry covering X", "have we never…", header fields and "why" questions go to the files, never to a cache (CACHES § When to use which store). A cache answer names its entry, and the node reads that entry before acting on it (Rd2). |
509:An engine's own built-in memory (Claude Code auto memory, a subagent's `memory` field, Codex memories) is not part of
515:Not verified: Codex's memory and subagent features beyond `AGENTS.md`.
